{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Toronto Blue Jays were a team that spent an entire season proving they belonged, only to watch the door close on them by the narrowest of margins. Built around a young, explosive core of Vladimir Guerrero Jr., Bo Bichette, and Marcus Semien — a free-agent acquisition who would redefine the position — Toronto posted 91 wins and scored 846 runs, numbers that in most years would have punched a playoff ticket. Instead, they were left watching October from home, victims of a brutal AL Wild Card race that offered no mercy.\n\nThe early months were a study in inconsistency. April ended at .500, and the bullpen — particularly Rafael Dolis and Joel Payamps — provided some of the season's most gut-wrenching moments. But the offense was already flashing its ceiling. Bo Bichette was launching home runs, and by late May, Guerrero and Semien were going back-to-back in the same game, signaling that this lineup could be something special. The team won 15 games in May despite a bullpen that occasionally imploded at the worst possible moments.\n\nJune brought more of the same electric offense — Bichette's walk-off triple, Guerrero's tape-measure shots, a lineup that simply refused to stop scoring. The Blue Jays hovered around .500 through the summer, going 12-11 in July and 16-14 in August, but George Springer's three-run blast on August 8th hinted at what this team could become once their leadoff man was fully healthy. Then September arrived, and everything clicked. Toronto went a scorching 19-9 in the final month, with Marcus Semien hitting a three-run walk-off homer on September 3rd to cap a wild comeback, and George Springer delivering a two-run shot on September 11th that carried the season's most valuable play by WPA. The Blue Jays won their final three games of the year, finishing 91-71 — and still missed the playoffs. It was a heartbreak that tasted like a promise.",
  "chapters": [
    {
